What Are Large Language Models?
Large Language Models (LLMs) are AI systems trained on very large text datasets using deep learning, mainly transformer architectures. They learn statistical patterns in language so they can understand prompts and generate coherent responses.

How Do They Work?
LLMs are trained with next-token prediction: given previous words, the model predicts the next one. This objective teaches grammar, context, structure, and many reasoning patterns from raw text without manual labels.

Before Transformers
Earlier sequence models like RNNs and LSTMs processed tokens one by one, which made long-range dependencies difficult and training less parallelizable. This limitation motivated transformer-based designs.

Transformers
Transformers use self-attention to compare each token with all relevant prior tokens in context. Multi-head attention captures different relationship types, and feed-forward blocks refine representations. This is the core method behind modern LLM capability.

Training Phase 1 (Pretraining)
Pretraining uses massive corpora (books, web, articles, code, dialogue). Data is cleaned, filtered, deduplicated, tokenized, and chunked. The model is then optimized to minimize next-token prediction loss over billions of tokens.

Post-Training and Alignment
After pretraining, instruction tuning and preference optimization improve helpfulness, format-following, and safety behavior. This stage turns a raw language predictor into a practical assistant.

Use Cases
- Chatbots and customer support
- Writing and summarization
- Coding assistance
- Search and information extraction
- Education and tutoring workflows

Ethical & Practical Challenges
- Bias from imbalanced training data
- Hallucinations (plausible but incorrect outputs)
- Privacy risks if sensitive text is memorized
- Misuse risks such as disinformation

